ion  we are this fourier transform of the  charge distribution  great in electron position scattering  there is another point it's not just the  extension of the proton  but also the fact that the proton  carries recoil  and so we uh need to use two form  factors in order to describe the  cross-section  um fully um so let's have a look at the  amplitude again we can  start from where we left off with the  electron  positron scattering or electron electron  neuron scattering  and write down our matrix elements and  just now for the proton  what we do here is we modify the vertex  of the proton  and the modification is parametrized in  those two factors  describing describing the the cross  section or the matrix elements amplitude  with two form factors  one which just looks like a modification  of our spin  half particle you remember there's a  scammer factor  and then you know this is going to be a  number  and the other one is a little bit more  complicated there is a  powder matrix here and another number  and it's normalized by the mass of the  proton all right so this is just the  parametrization we haven't done much  physics here we have just parametrized  the distribution um if you then use this  we can as i just described um  calculate the cross section again using  those this very same parametrization and  get  to this formula here in the laboratory  frame  um this looks rather complicated but  if you uh go back to our form factor  definitions  and set this one the zero and this one  to one you get back in the same here  you said get back to our uh much  scattering result which we had before so  really extended  the discussion to extended extended  objects  and considering the charge distribution  and also the recall in the quarter  so this is great um historically  so this is not a new idea this has been  done for generations  historically the parametrization was  done slightly different  and so we introduced the linear  combination of those form factors and  those are  typically referred to as the electric  and the magnetic four factors the ge and  gm  this formula here and this is just  algebra going from  previous formula to this it's called the  rosen  blues thousand blues  um cross-section formula all right so  this is just if you find this in his in  in  particle physics booklets or nuclear  physics booklet  this is what's meant by this the only  thing we did here is  i extended the rosen the mod scattering  formula  using extended objects extend the charge  distribution to the  formula all right but  we have done that by using a full  transform of the charge distribution so  if we now measure the cross section  we can infer the charge distribution of  the total  and with that the radius the charge  radius of the proton this has been done  and we find that the rms the root mean  square of the proton charge is 0.81  femtometers uh so that's the charge and  this is  this measured charge is still today uh a  hot topic in particle physics because  it's measured distributions do not quite  agree with  the theory predictions so you see this  here parametrized  for this value of the put in charge the  theory and the global fit to the data  don't quite agree  you would have to go to slightly higher  values of the proton radius in order to  have  theory the previous experiments  so i leave it here the next lecture now  i  split this lecture in two parts the  elastic and the inelastic scattering the  next lecture will be  on inelastic scattering where we will  break the proton apart and learn about  the structure of the proton  you

